public_description:
To whom it may concern;
I live in Lincoln Military housing at the corner of Garnet and Pendleton .There is a Collision body shop directly across the street from my home. They are constantly taking up the entire public street in order to park the cars that they are working on . They are left for days . The people who reside on this street have ZERO place to park on the street . They constantly park in front of our driveways and move our trash cans on Friday so they can park cars where we placed our cans. I have read the San Diego Municipal Code Chapter 8: Traffic and Vehicles section and according to it .       2.	(b)  It is unlawful for any person to leave standing or park any vehicle upon any street for the purpose of servicing or repairing such vehicle, except in an emergency.       4.	(d)  It is unlawful for any person who deals in, or whose business involves the sale, lease, rental, or charter of vehicles to store, park, or leave standing any such vehicle upon any public street, except while such vehicle is under lease, rental, or charter by a customer.  So according to your municipal code what they are doing is illegal . I am just tired of not having a place to park and I live here. I am not the only person in this area who is upset. I have uploaded several pictures for you to take into consideration . I also believe that there should be  a RED no parking zone  closer to  the residents driveways so people do not block them.  Thank you for your help. I do hope this issue  is resolved. 
Signed an upset resident . Please feel free to contact me anytime. Dana Clark - 904-424-0459

Business: PB Avenue
Observed Waste Mgmt. Trash only; 
Service Levels: 1  3cyd picked up 2 x a week; Mon&Fri.

Please schedule the removal of at least 2 very large hazardous pine trees directly behind our home (4387 Mistral Place, San Diego 92130) and our neighbor?s home (4395 Mistral Place) in Carmel Valley, on a steep slope on the north side of Carmel Country Road.

Two of these flanking trees are located only 8.5 feet from our cinderblock fence line, with the central one about 13? away.  These trees:
?	are top heavy and swayed a great deal during recent winds and storms, constituting a significant danger to pedestrians and drivers going east on Carmel Country Road;
?	generate large amounts of highly flammable pine needles that are extremely dangerous during fire season;
?	significantly shade our solar panels installed in 2015, reducing their power output;
?	block growth of our palm trees within the fence line; and
?	damaged our backyard fence through their root system.  This tree-damaged fence recently required an expensive repair.

Several photos are attached.

